## 0. Routing — new recipient token `designer`

Dean established this token today. **`designer`** = the session that owns the Type 1
(`combined-analyzer-optimizer-design.md`). It is *not* the PR-2 internal code reviewer and *not* a
working planner.

**Correcting my own first read of why this was needed.** I initially described the existing traffic
as misrouted to the wrong session. It is not, and the fault is mine rather than the planner's. A
header sweep across the nine relevant files says:

- The planner sent Type-1 *questions* to `review` — `review__ta-anchor-type1-fairshare-floor-vs-eviction.md`
  and `review__ta-anchor-type1-row7-contradiction.md`, both a bare `to: review`. **That was the right
  recipient.** Both asked whether a claim holds against the code, which is adjudication; the reviewer
  did the work and answered with `plan__ta-anchor-type1-verdicts.md` (*"type-1 verdicts — fairShareCap
  rounding, row-7 contradiction"*). Nothing was misdelivered and nothing was wasted.
- Three others carry the fused `to: review (PR-2 internal code reviewer / Type-1 owner)`. The
  *destination* is still right; the parenthetical asserts a false ownership. The reviewer caught
  exactly that — *"both handoffs address me as 'Type-1 owner' — I am the internal code reviewer"* —
  then adjudicated everything adjudicable and declined only the item that was a **decision** rather
  than a **verdict** (the claim-pricing disposition).
- The genuinely misrouted class is **outbound**: three files addressed
  `to: planner (Type-1 owner for combined-analyzer-optimizer-design.md)` — two from review, one from
  the coder. `planner` is the Type-3 owner and cannot make those calls, which is why the coder's
  `(D-a)`-cannot-ship finding sits `.WIP` under a claimant who can only relay it onward.
- **Where the false belief came from is my own `from:` line.** `plan__ta-anchor-c11-ranking-claim-correction.md`
  reads `from: plans (Type-1 owner / PR-2 Type-3 review session)` — one session self-labelled with two
  roles — and the same fusion then shows up in everyone else's headers. Two further variants of mine:
  `plan-review session (anchor-refactor Type-1 owner)` ×2. **If a handoff confused the planner, that
  is the one**, and it is not the planner's error.

So the fix is not re-addressing traffic. It is (i) a token for this role, and (ii) splitting
*verdict* asks from *decision* asks instead of bundling both into one handoff.

Going forward: **verification** of a Type-1 claim against the code still goes to `review` — that
worked well and produced real verdicts. **Amendments and decisions** on the frozen Type 1 go to
`designer__<topic>.md`. `planner` stays the Type-3 owner. My outbound `from:` line is a bare
`designer` from here on.

**On `PENDING-EDITS`.** `plan__ta-anchor-c11-da-sentinel-unsized.md` cites *"your `PENDING-EDITS`
rows A1–A5"* and *"row A6"*. **No such ledger exists** — a full-tree grep finds exactly two hits,
both inside that one handoff. The coder is reasoning against an artifact it believes I publish; my
queue has been conversation state only. §5 below is the real queue, with fresh row IDs (`T1-n`)
rather than an invented `A1–A6` mapping. If the coder's `A1–A6` referred to something specific it
should say what, because it cannot have read it.

