## 1. `(D-a)`'s stated population is wrong, and the discriminator is free

`(D-a)`'s justification, now also written into production prose at `analyzer_helpers.go:280-286`,
is that a variant reaching the merge's binder-miss branch at zero replicas *"is a variant nobody has
ever measured"*, because previously-live variants are covered by TA's own scale-from-zero
complement. **That reasoning has a hole on each side.**

**TA side — the complement lapses.** The complement (`throughput/analyzer.go:385-414`) emits a
PRC-only capacity only when `st.lastPerReplicaSupply > 0` for a persisted `variantStates` entry, and
those entries are **deleted** after `2*DefaultObservationMaxAge` of non-observation
(`throughput/analyzer.go:156-163`). TA's own comment concedes the consequence: *"a long-idle variant
degrades to the never-seen case."* So a variant that scaled to zero and stayed there past that
window reaches the branch **having been measured**.

**Saturation side — a measured number still exists for exactly that variant.** For a variant with no
ready replicas, saturation resolves capacity in three tiers
(`saturation_v2/analyzer.go:421-432`):

| tier | source | `Reason` label |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | own `capacityStore` record with `EffectiveCapacity > 0` → `estimateStoredCapacity` | `satReasonP0Store` |
| 2 | `lookupCompatibleCapacity` — a compatible sibling variant's record | `satReasonP0Store` |
| 3 | nothing | `satReasonNoData` |

`estimateStoredCapacity` returns the stored `EffectiveCapacity` **directly and authoritatively** when
`rec.LearnedFrom == learnedFromLive` (`:536-538`, *"Live records have observed capacity — use
directly"*), and live records are protected from being clobbered by deployment-derived writes
(`capacity_store.go:99-101`, *"Don't overwrite live data"*). The value and its label are emitted on
the entry (`:448`, `:452`).

**So three populations reach that branch, not one:**

| population | state | is there a measured PRC? |
|---|---|---|
| **lapsed** | ran, scaled to zero, TA state expired | **yes** — own `learnedFromLive` record |
| **cold-but-priceable** | never ran; sibling or deployment-derived estimate exists | **yes**, an estimate |
| **cold** | never ran, no record at all | **no** — `satReasonNoData` |

**The discriminator is already in hand at the write site.** `bindingAnchor` locates the identity
carrier **by name, with no `Enabled` or `Live` check** (`analyzer_helpers.go:202`, then
`:237-239`), so `a.Reason` is available on every iteration of the merge loop in every
configuration — `P0-store` vs `no-data` separates tiers 1–2 from tier 3, and one level deeper
`LearnedFrom == "live"` separates lapsed from cold-but-priceable. No new plumbing, no new state, no
new currency. The merge currently **discards it**: `out.Reason` is assigned only inside the
`if b, ok := bByName[...]` hit branch (`:274-276`), so the else branch drops `a.Reason` on the floor.

Incidentally the reason that field is universally available is the known **F1 gap** — saturation runs
and emits real capacities even under `saturation:{enabled:false}`; only its `Enabled` tag goes false.
The bug that makes disabling saturation a no-op is what makes this discriminator reliable, which is
worth remembering if F1 is ever fixed.

---

## 2. Reconciliation with review Finding 44 — we do not disagree

Finding 44 (`cf94a36d`) enumerates the sentinel branch's reachable domain and concludes `(D-a)`
as specified cannot scale a variant from zero in **any** configuration. **I endorse that.** It closes
the *mechanism* question harder than the coder's single fixture did, and it correctly rules out the
narrowing a reader would reach for first (*"only admit when saturation votes"* — unreachable, because
sat's voting predicate is also its binding predicate).

What my finding adds is that its middle column — *"variant priced by any voter?"* — answers a
question about the **vote gate**, not about whether a price exists:

| sub-case | Finding 44 | is there a measured PRC? | why it does not reach the math |
|---|---|---|---|
| sat `!Enabled` | abstains → 0 | **yes** (P0-store) | operator disabled saturation — correctly refused |
| sat `Enabled && !Live` | abstains → 0 | **yes** (P0-store, often `learnedFromLive`) | **excluded by the liveness gate** (`votingResults:315-318`) |
| sat `Enabled && Live && !Informative` | PRC-0 rows, gates skip → 0 | **no** | genuinely nothing to use |

Only sub-case 3 is *unsizable*. **Sub-case 2 — which is the coder's measured regression fixture — is
sizable-but-gated:** a number measured on that very variant while it was running, sitting in
saturation's own ballot entry, excluded on liveness grounds. So "cannot scale from zero in any
configuration" is right about the sentinel and overstates the data situation in the one sub-case that
carries the measurement.

**Two consequences.**

**(a) The amendment splits into two questions, not one.**

1. May the anchor size a binder-omitted variant from the **identity carrier's stored capacity** when
   the carrier is enabled-but-stale? That is an `N8` / `VG-up` question — the liveness gate's scope —
   and it is mine. It is *not* a `(D-a)` question, and `(D-a)`'s deferral does not dispose of it.
2. What happens to a genuinely never-measured variant (`Reason == no-data`)? That is the real
   `(D-a)`, on a domain two populations smaller than the frozen text claims.

**(b) A narrowing that Finding 44 does not close.** Its consequence #1 rules out *"admit only when
saturation votes"*. My discriminator does not require saturation to vote — the carrier is located by
name, so `a.Reason` is readable in all three sub-cases. *"Admit only when the carrier has no record"*
is a **different** narrowing and survives the reachability argument. Please do not let the two be
conflated in the plan; they look similar and are not.

**Option ranking.** I agree option 2 (floor `roleBottleneckReplicas` at 1 for a tagged variant) is
the weakest, and Finding 44's sub-case-3 argument is the sharper reason. A second, independent
reason: in sub-case 2 the floor would fabricate a grant while a real measured PRC sat available and
ignored — wrong in the one configuration where nothing needed inventing. Option 1 (binder emits the
sentinel into its own ballot entry) stays live and becomes *scopeable* under the `no-data` predicate,
at the cost of a synthetic value entering the vote, which is the `N8` question proper. Option 3
(defer) is what the tree reflects today, and it is a legitimate answer — but it should be recorded as
a **decision**, not as the default the coder reached by elimination.

